and designation of SMP', '    Ÿ Consequences of SMP - remedies', '[pic]', 'What is Significant Market Power – SMP?', '    Ÿ Concept of SMP is redefined. “Old” definition of SMP is based on 25% share of the relevant market + additional discretionary criteria. The “New” definition of SMP is based on the concept of dominant position, i.e. the based on doctrines under competition law (40%)', '    Ÿ Transition to competition law methodology. In practical terms: Ex ante regulation will be applied to former monopolists and/or those who control essential facilities (recital 20)', '    Ÿ SMP is not equal to dominance. The competition authorities are not bound by NRAs decisions', 'SMP as a regulatory tool', '    Ÿ SMP is a ”limit” for application of several obligations (Remedies) and represents the limit for when NRAs can apply regulation.', '    Ÿ Ex-ante regulation shall only be applied in certain circumstances: i.e on operators with SMP (see art. 16 of FD and art. 8 of AD)', '    Ÿ NRA cannot place obligations on undertakings without SMP', '    Ÿ Certain obligations (lighter) can be placed on all undertakings (se AD art. 4 and 5 and USO chapter IV.)', '    Ÿ SMP must be defined ex ante (FD art 16 (4))', '    o tresholds for definitions', '    o NRA also indicates elements that may be used for assessing appropriate obligations', '[pic]', '[pic]', 'Internal and external relations', '[pic]', 'THREE STEP PROCEDURE for defining smp', '    Ÿ (See Guidelines recital 9)', '1. Defining the relevant markets', '2. Market analysis and SMP', '    Ÿ 2 A: Analyse the markets', '    Ÿ 2 B: Determining who has SMP', '3. Determining proportionate obligations for the SMP undertaking', '    Ÿ to ensure that undertakings cannot use their market power to restrict, to distort competition, or to leverage such market power to adjacent markets (see Guidelines recital 16)', 'STEP 1: defining the Relevant markets', '1 Market definition procedure', '    Ÿ (Art 15 of the FD) describes the methodology for defining SMP', '    Ÿ Principles for market definition. Commission has determined 18 product and service markets. Art 15 + Annex I of the Framework Directive identifies initial  markets', '    Ÿ Commission Recommendation on Relevant Product and Service Markets (hereinafter ""the Recommendation"") describes 18 markets', '    Ÿ NRAs will determine the geographical markets', '    Ÿ Market definition shall be done in accordance with the principles of competition law – as opposed to earlier markets (based on end-to-end connectivity rather than demand and supply-side criteria).', '2 Art 15 of the FD:', '   1. \tAfter public consultation and consultation with national regulatory authorities the Commission shall adopt a recommendation on relevant product and service markets (hereinafter “the recommendation”). The recommendation shall identify in accordance with Annex I hereto those product and service markets within the electronic communications sector, the characteristics of which may be such as to justify the imposition of regulatory obligations set out in the Specific Directives, without prejudice to markets that may be defined in specific cases under competition law. The Commission shall define markets in accordance with the principles of competition law. The Commission shall regularly review the recommendation.', '   2. \tThe Commission shall publish, at the latest on the date of entry into force of this Directive, guidelines for market analysis and the assessment of significant market power (hereinafter “the guidelines”) which shall be in accordance with the principles of competition law.', '   3. \tNational regulatory authorities shall, taking the utmost account of the recommendation and the guidelines, define relevant markets appropriate to national circumstances, in particular relevant geographic markets within their territory, in accordance with the principles of competition law. National regulatory authorities shall follow the procedures referred to in Articles 6 and 7 before defining the markets that differ from those defined in the recommendation..', '   4. \tAfter consultation with national regulatory authorities the Commission may, acting in accordance with the procedure referred to in Article 22(3), adopt a Decision identifying transnational markets.', '    Ÿ Market definition procedure is prospective rather than behavioural and is based on assessment of barriers to entry that are expected to last.', '3 The main criteria are linked to substation tests:', '    Ÿ Demand side substitution (i.e Ramsey pricing): Are consumers prepared to substitute other services for the relevant service? Significant non-transitory increase in price (SNIIP) – shift in demand?', '    Ÿ Supply side substitution  (quick responses from competitors): Would a supplier of other services switch to “compete” immediately or in the short term without incurring significant additional costs?', '    Ÿ Potential competition (more time for competitors to respond)', '    Ÿ Geographical markets are determined based on (i) Areas covered by a network and (ii) Existence of legal/regulatory instruments', '    Ÿ The Recommendation identifies markets in which ex ante regulations may be warranted (recital 3)', '    Ÿ If no effective competition (one undertaking in a single or jointly dominant position) (recital 4)', '    Ÿ Annex to recommendations indicated how each market is linked to the market areas in annex I to FD.', '    Ÿ The identified markets have high and non-transitory entry barriers (recital 8)', '      Ÿ Structural barriers (cost, demand conditions, economies of scale/scope, network components that cannot be duplicated or duplication is uneconomic) (recital  10)', '      Ÿ legal/regulatory barriers (recital 11)', '      Ÿ expected to persist over a foreseeable period (recital 13)', '4 The Current relevant markets', '    Ÿ [pic]', '5 Further segmentation of retail markets', '[pic]', 'STEP 2 A: Market analysis', '    Ÿ Legal instruments: Art. 16 and Guidelines', '    Ÿ “As soon as possible after the adoption of the recommendation”', '    Ÿ NRA shall determine whether a relevant market is effectively competitive (art 16 (2))', '    Ÿ Step 2 B and 3 depends on the state of the market', '    Ÿ Is the relevant market effectively competitive?', '1 STEP 2 A: Outcome depends on competition', '    Ÿ If the market IS effectively competitive:', '    o NRA shall not impose or maintain obligations in art. 16 (2)', '    o NRA shall withdraw existing obligations after an appropriate period', '    Ÿ If a market is NOT effectively competitive', '    o NRA’s shall identify undertakings with SMP', '    o Art. 14 applies + Guidelines', '    o Impose obligations', '    o NRA shall at least impose one obligation (art. 16 (4))', '    o May impose several obligations', '    o Maintain or amend existing obligations', '2 Step 2A: When is the market competitive?', '    Ÿ When no operator enjoys a single or joint dominance on the relevant market', '    Ÿ i.e. when no operator has significant market power', '3 Step 2 A: Principles', '    Ÿ Forward looking', '    Ÿ Structural evaluation', '    Ÿ Based on the existing market conditions', '    Ÿ Is the market prospectively competitive?', '    Ÿ Is any lack of competition durable?', '    Ÿ Expected market developments over a reasonable period', '    o specific characteristics (new licenses)', '    o expected timing for the next review', '    Ÿ Take past data into account', '4 STEP 2 B: Defining SMP (dominance)', '    Ÿ Definition of SMP in art. 14 FD', '    o the same as definition of “Dominant Position”', '    o Dominant Position developed in case law', '    o European Court of Justice (ECJ) and Court of First Instance (CFI) applies under art. 81 and 82 of the Treaty (FD preamble recital 20 and Guidelines recital 62)', '    Ÿ NRA must ensure that they apply the definition consistently with ECJ and CFI case law', '    Ÿ Different methodology:', '    o Different sets of assumptions and expectations', '    o Lack of evidence of past behaviour', '    o prospective instead of retrospective approach', '    Ÿ Does not mean that there is no competition (Guidelines recital 72)', '    Ÿ Power to raise prices by restricting output without incurring significant loss of sales or revenues (Guidelines recital 73)', '    Ÿ “Appreciable effect on competition“', '    Ÿ Likelihood of potential competition (Guidelines recital 74)', '    Ÿ Alternatives:', '    o One undertaking in one market', '    o Several undertakings in one market (joint dominance)', '    o One undertaking in several markets (leveraging)', '5 FD Art. 14 Undertakings with SMP', '   1. \tWhere the Specific Directives require national regulatory authorities to determine whether operators have significant market power in accordance with the procedure referred to in Article 16, paragraphs 2 and 3 of this Article shall apply.', '   2. \tAn undertaking shall be deemed to have significant market power if, either individually or jointly with others, it enjoys a position equivalent to dominance, that is to say a position of economic strength affording it the power to behave to an appreciable extent independently of competitors, customers and ultimately consumers.', '       \tIn particular, national regulatory authorities shall, when assessing whether two or more undertakings are in a joint dominant position in a market, act in accordance with Community law and take into the utmost account the Guidelines on market analysis and the assessment of significant market power published by the Commission pursuant to Article 15. Criteria to be used in making such an assessment are set out in Annex II.', '   3. \tWhere an undertaking has significant market power on a specific market, it may also be deemed to have significant market power on a closely related market, where the links between the two markets are such as to allow the market power held in one market to be leveraged into the other market, thereby strengthening the market power of the undertaking.', '6 Step 2B: Criterias for SMP (Guidelines recital 75-82)', '    Ÿ Rule of thumb – market shares (stable over time)', '    o Under 25% - unlikely single dominance', '    o Between 25 and 40 – uncertain', '    o Over 40% - normally and likely single dominance', '    o Over 50% - always (save in exceptional circumstances)', '    Ÿ Measuring market share', '    o Volume (i.e. minutes)', '    o Revenue (i.e. interconnection)', '    Ÿ See Guidelines recital 75-77', '    Ÿ Market shares alone does not mean SMP', '    Ÿ NRA must therefore analyse the economic characteristics of the markets', '    Ÿ Several criterias must be assessed (See Guidelines recital 78)', '7 Step 2B Criterias to be assessed (Guidelines recital 78)', '    Ÿ overall size of the undertaking', '    Ÿ control of infrastructure not easily duplicated', '    Ÿ technological advantages or superiority', '    Ÿ absence of or low countervailing buying power', '    Ÿ easy or privileged access to capital markets/financial resources', '    Ÿ product/services diversification e.g. bundled products or services', '    Ÿ economies of scale e.g. large customer base', '    Ÿ economies of scope', '    Ÿ diversified services', '    Ÿ vertical integration', '    Ÿ a highly developed distribution and sales network', '    Ÿ absence of potential competition', '8 2B: Findings and evaluation', '    Ÿ Ease of market entry', '    o Absence of barriers to entry may mean potential competition', '    o Barriers are often high', '    o legal (licensing)', '    o large investments and capacity (man power)', '    Ÿ Essential facilities', '    o No relevant jurisprudence in the EC sector', '    o Does the control of a facility mean that the operator has significant market power, not to establish that the facility can be considered ”essential” or ”indispensable” under existing case-law (Bronner, Night Services, etc).', '9 Step 2B: Leveraging (Guidelines 3.1.1)', '    Ÿ SMP in one market may be used to dominate another closely related market (adjacent market) where the operator does not have SMP (FD art. 14.)', '    Ÿ Criteria used in the assessment:', '    o Horizontal links', '    o Close and associative links', '    o Often found in vertically integrated markets (the case in the telecom sector)', '    o If an operator has SMP on the upstream (wholesale or access) market the NRAs will normally prevent a spill over to downstream markets (retail).', '    Ÿ It is only when ex ante obligations on the SMP operator in the upstream market does not result in effective competition in the retail markets (downstream) that art. 14 (3) may apply (Guidelines recital 84)', '10 Step 2B: Collective dominance', '    Ÿ mature market', '    Ÿ stagnant or moderate growth on the demand side', '    Ÿ low elasticity of demand', '    Ÿ homogeneous product', '    Ÿ similar cost structures', '    Ÿ similar market shares', '    Ÿ lack of technical innovation, mature technology', '    Ÿ absence of excess capacity', '    Ÿ high barriers to entry', '    Ÿ lack of countervailing buying power', '    Ÿ lack of potential competition', '    Ÿ various kind of informal or other links between the undertakings concerned 94', '    Ÿ retaliatory mechanisms', '    Ÿ lack or reduced scope for price competition', 'Summary', '    Ÿ More competition law – NRAs must adhere to competition law principles', '    Ÿ Relevant Product and Service markets', '    Ÿ Basic: Geographical markets', '    Ÿ More flexible (dynamic instead of static)', '    Ÿ Annual assessment necessary – focus on lifting regulation when competition is sufficient', '1 Step 3: Consequences of SMP - Remedies', '    Ÿ FD art. 16 (4): NRAs ”shall on such undertakings with SMP impose appropriate specific regulatory obligations”', '    Ÿ  FD art 16 (2): Refers to USO D art. 16, 17, 18 or 19. Refers to AD art 7 and 8', '    Ÿ ”impose, maintain, amend or withdraw obligations on undertakings”', '2 Important remedies', '    Ÿ Transparency (art. 9)', '    Ÿ Non-discrimination (art. 10)', '    Ÿ Accounting separation (Art. 11)', '    Ÿ Price control and accounting obligations (art. 13)']"
